title start
kind: research
tags: research
Research page: a question, how you tested it, what you found
title end

paragraph start
Use a research page when you investigate one question and want a reader to find the method and the results without reading everything. Replace each section text with your own.
paragraph end

section start
title start
Abstract
title end

paragraph start
Summarise the whole page in a few sentences: the question, the method, the result and one limit. Write it last. A reader who stops here should still know what you found.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Introduction
title end

paragraph start
Say what the question is and why it matters. Name what is already known and what is missing, so the reader sees where your work fits.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Methods
title end

paragraph start
Describe what you did so a reader could repeat it from this text alone: the material, the steps and the tools. Put commands or code in a code block.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Results
title end

paragraph start
Report what you found, without opinion. Every claim must point to evidence such as a number, a quote or a file. Leave the meaning for the next section.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Discussion
title end

paragraph start
Say what the results mean, what they do not show, and what to test next. Be honest about limits and about anything that surprised you.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
References
title end

paragraph start
List every source you cited, one per line, with enough detail to find it again. A source in the text must appear here.
paragraph end

section end

comment start
abstract, introduction, methods, results, discussion, references
comment end

